(I'm having difficulty parsing your last sentence there.  Are you
saying that it's okay that G/SDSM is better than all the other colors
and armors, or that DSMs are better than all the other armors?)

Silver and Gray are still incontrovertibly better than not just all the
other colors of DSM, but all the other armor in the game.

I'm unhappy, in general, about the idea of useless items, although some
of them are more or less unavoidable.  In Rogue (a game that, in some
respects, is superior to Nethack in design), players shouldn't discard
armor willy-nilly unless they have a good protected set (if protection
scrolls are in that version) or are out of rust monster/aquator
territory.  When armor was made less disposable (and so tremendously
heavy), the main use for all the armor that gets generated in a game
vanished.  Now, I can't remember when I chose to wear non-DSM armor
other than leather or a character's starting suit.

DSM's problem isn't that it's got the highest AC, it's that it's almost
completely devoid of disadvantages: They're among the lightest if not
THE lightest armor, they have the best AC, they don't erode, they don't
restrict spellcasting AND they provide an additional intrinsic in
addition to all these things.  It is made from dragon scales instead of
found randomly so even most wishless games get it eventually.  It even
provides a powerful, automatic polymorph form!  The only thing it sucks
at is providing magic cancellation.

In Nethack, there are all these legacy Rogue items whose original uses
have been long made irrelevant by powergaming items like DSM.  Almost
all of the original armor is like this now: who wears plate mail if
they can help it?  Or mithril?  In Rogue, leather armor is the weakest
type, but it turns out to still be useful, indeed very useful in some
cases, because it's immune to rust traps and the attacks of aquators.
That's the kind of thing I'm suggesting be done with Nethack's legacy
armors.

(This opinion is partly a reaction to my own frequently-offered
suggestion to new players that GDSM is the definitive first wish they
should make.)

> It would be kind of cool to have shimmering DSM, or the proposed gold
> DSM, or other useful types.  But there's nothing wrong with some kinds
> being clearly inferior, IMO.

It's not so much a case of something be wrong as a sense that it's a
*missed opportunity to add strategy to the game*.  There are lots of
things like that in Nethack.  Many of the features in the old variant
Nethack+ seemed to be attempts to make good on them.

